[ -- Command

Evaluate a conditional expression -- the bracket spelling of test
[ expression ]

/bin/[ is /bin/test under a second name.  It is the same program, staged twice
by the distribution, and it evaluates exactly the expressions test evaluates
and returns exactly the same status: 0 if the expression is true, 1 if it is
false, and 2 with the expression named on standard error if the expression
cannot be read at all.  See test for the operators.

The one difference is the argument list.  The program looks at the last
character of the name it was invoked under: if that character is `[', a final
argument of `]' is required and is removed before the expression is evaluated.
Under any other name no bracket is expected, and a `]' on the end of the line
would be an operand like any other.  So

    if [ -f /etc/nologin ]
    if test -f /etc/nologin

are the same test, and

    if [ -f /etc/nologin

is a usage error rather than a condition that did not hold.

THE BRACKETS ARE NOT SHELL SYNTAX.  `[' is a command name and `]' is an
argument, so both need white space around them, and an operand that might be
empty or might begin with `-' needs quoting, exactly as it does for test:

    if [ "$answer" = yes ]

The Bourne shell here has no built-in `['; every use of it runs this program.

Files
/bin/[		this program
/bin/test	the same program under its own name

Notes
This page has no COHERENT Lexicon article of its own.  The Lexicon's test
article mentions in passing that `[' is linked to test, but the index has no
entry under `[', so `man [' found nothing on a machine that ships the command.
This page was written here to answer it; none of Mark Williams Company's text
is in it.

